# {{.SiteName}}

Reusable, white-label documentation built with `fastr-docs` and GoFastr.

## Run it

```sh
fastr-docs check .
fastr-docs doctor .
fastr-docs build .
fastr-docs dev .
# run once without the watcher
go run .
# or export a static, installable PWA
fastr-docs export . --out dist
# optional chunked static search
fastr-docs export . --out dist --pagefind
```

`fastr-docs dev` uses GoFastr's default <http://localhost:8080>. Pass
`--addr localhost:3070` when you need a different port. Running `go run .`
without the watcher uses the app's default <http://localhost:3070>.

`fastr-docs dev` watches Go, Markdown, HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and JSON/YAML
contract files. After each successful rebuild it restarts the server and
refreshes open browser tabs. Use `go run .` for a single process without file
watching.

`fastr-docs dev` is the fastr-docs wrapper around GoFastr's CLI. It adds
contract-file watching for JSON/YAML inputs. If you install GoFastr separately,
`gofastr dev` runs the framework server directly and watches its native file
set.

`fastr-docs build .` runs GoFastr's build gates and compiler. Use
`fastr-docs upgrade . --apply` to apply GoFastr framework migrations through
the same project wrapper.

The generated host is installable as a PWA on `localhost` and on HTTPS. A live
install keeps the app shell and offline screen available; use the static
export when every published document must be available offline:

```sh
fastr-docs export . --out dist --pagefind
```

Each static export receives a content-fingerprinted GoFastr worker cache. A
redeploy installs the new worker in the background and activates it after old
tabs close, so new tabs receive the updated docs without interrupting the
current session.

To update the GoFastr dependency and its migration notes, use the fastr-docs
wrapper (or call the GoFastr CLI directly):

```sh
fastr-docs upgrade .
fastr-docs upgrade . --apply
```

Keep the installed `gofastr` CLI on the same release as the module in
`go.mod`.

## Where to edit

- `docs/router.go` is the central route tree.
- `content/` contains Markdown pages.
- `content/blog/` contains optional publication posts. `MarkdownBlog` builds the archive and RSS feed from these files.
- `openapi.json` feeds the in-project OpenAPI reference plugin.
- `API_SERVER_URL` optionally overrides the OpenAPI contract's default server for staging or production.
- `public/` contains white-label assets served at `/assets/` and copied into static exports.
- `agents/claude.md` and `.agents/skills/` describe the project conventions to AI agents.

The live host serves GoFastr's read-only MCP introspection endpoint at `/mcp`
and advertises it through the agent card and `/.well-known/mcp.json`. Static
exports include the docs and discovery files, but cannot serve the live MCP
transport.

## Content model

Markdown files may start with YAML front matter. Supported fields include
`title`, `description`, `draft`, `noindex`, `edit_url`, `canonical`, `image`,
`authors`, `date`, `last_updated`, `locale`, `version`, `translation_of`,
`alternates`, `tags`, `redirects`, and `order`. Front matter is removed before rendering and search.

Markdown can also embed registered typed components:

```md
{{"{{"}}< callout title="Important" >}}
This remains Markdown inside a reusable GoFastr callout.
{{"{{"}}< /callout >}}
```

Shared components are registered in `docs/router.go` with
`MarkdownComponentsPlugin`; page-local components can be supplied through
`PageConfig.Components`.

Route groups, pages, and screens can also set a short `Badge` for sidebar
metadata such as `New`, `Popular`, or `Beta`. Badges appear in both the
desktop rail and mobile drawer without changing the route or search record.

For larger sites, register a directory with `router.MarkdownCollection` so
the route tree, ordering, locale/version filters, drafts, redirects, and
search metadata stay derived from the same files. Set `DOCS_INCLUDE_DRAFTS=1`
for local review. `DOCS_LOCALE` sets the document language while
`DOCS_CONTENT_LOCALE` / `DOCS_CONTENT_VERSION` select a build-time content
slice; leaving the content filters empty keeps runtime locale/version
switchers available.

`PUBLIC_SITE_URL` configures absolute sitemap URLs. `API_SERVER_URL` controls
the OpenAPI request origin. Use `fastr-docs export --base /docs` when the
static site is deployed below a path prefix.

The generated project includes a Router-backed blog at `/blog` and an RSS
feed at `/blog/feed.xml`. Set `PUBLIC_SITE_URL` in production so feed links
are absolute. Remove the sample post and directory if the project does not
publish updates.

The shell is composed from GoFastr UI primitives. The generated `main.go`
mounts the framework-owned mobile sidebar drawer and native command palette;
the primary header reflects top-level route groups and `Meta/Control + K`
opens route search. Extend the header, navigation, document layout, and
scrollspy through GoFastr components rather than adding a parallel interaction
layer. If the project registers localized or versioned route siblings, the
header also provides route-aware Language and Version selectors.

Use `fastr-docs export --pagefind` for the optional Pagefind static search
bundle. Development and exports without that flag use the portable JSON index.
